{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgEnvUtQ6iGyoTdE56by8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed the bug, and all 5 tests pass. I ran them with `PYTHONPATH=. python3 -m unittest tests.test_inventory -v`. Plain `unittest discover` fails here because `tests/` has no `__init__.py`, which is unrelated to this fix.\n\n**The fix:** in `inventory.py`, `parse_price` now treats commas as thousands separators when the string matches one of two patterns. The first is one to three digits, then comma groups of exactly three digits, then a decimal part (`1,234.50`). The second is two or more comma groups with no dot (`1,234,567`). Anything else containing a comma still gets the European decimal-comma handling, so `\"12,50\"` → `12.5` still works. `\"12.50\"` and `\"$3\"` also still pass, and `total_value` is unchanged.\n\n**Regression test:** I added `test_thousands_separator`. It checks `\"1,234.50\"` → `1234.5` and `\"$1,234,567\"` → `1234567.0`.\n\nA bare `\"1,234\"` with no dot or second comma group is still read as a decimal comma, giving `1.234`. That follows the rule in `ISSUE.md`, but it is ambiguous, so say so if you want it treated as 1234.\n\nI haven't committed anything."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":329,"cache_read_input_tokens":25108,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":329},"output_tokens":421,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9c8f95c6-f438-45ab-9a89-6fb71bc13985","uuid":"993d3189-ed27-47ce-a3b4-de18cba66e65","timestamp":"2026-10-21T04:59:52.719Z","request_id":"req_011CgEnvUFCyFNPyVdEJuHbJ"}
